WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.447
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.447 --> 00:00:10.826
I grew up in a family
with two amazing moms.

00:00:10.826 --> 00:00:16.865
My mom Jane gave birth to my brother
Jonah, and my mom Deb gave birth to me.

00:00:16.865 --> 00:00:20.292
This is a photo of all of
us at their wedding day,

00:00:20.292 --> 00:00:24.336
after marriage equality was
passed in Massachusetts.

00:00:24.336 --> 00:00:31.313
But even growing up in liberal Boston,
our family structure was still unusual.

00:00:31.313 --> 00:00:35.102
To some, it was unnatural.

00:00:35.102 --> 00:00:39.257
People even questioned whether
we were a real family.

00:00:39.257 --> 00:00:43.719
They would say things like,
isn't it like weird that you and

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:47.145
your brother aren't biologically related?

00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:50.176
No, he's my brother.

00:00:50.176 --> 00:00:52.900
Do you wish you knew your dad?

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.171
No, I've never thought of my
parents sperm donor as my dad.

00:00:58.171 --> 00:01:03.066
But like,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:03.066 --> 00:01:06.148
&gt;&gt; Do you think you might be gay because

00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:08.111
your parents are gay?

00:01:08.111 --> 00:01:11.050
Well, I don't know.

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.905
Those first two questions were easy,
because our family's love,

00:01:15.905 --> 00:01:19.919
definitively, did not depend
on genetic connection.

00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:22.308
But they were frustrating, and

00:01:22.308 --> 00:01:28.155
constant reminders of how important
genetic connection was to other people.

00:01:28.155 --> 00:01:31.100
But that last question
really got me thinking.

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:37.223
Because by the time I was 17 years old,
even though I had a boyfriend,

00:01:37.223 --> 00:01:42.341
I had kind of started to develop
a crush on a girl in my school.

00:01:42.341 --> 00:01:45.538
So I thought, am I?

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:48.737
And if so, why?

00:01:48.737 --> 00:01:52.591
So I set out in search of an explanation.

00:01:52.591 --> 00:01:56.331
I opened my computer, and

00:01:56.331 --> 00:02:02.111
typed into Google two words, gay gene.

00:02:02.111 --> 00:02:05.463
That Google search for an explanation for

00:02:05.463 --> 00:02:11.028
my feelings launched a ten year
journey of learning about genetics.

00:02:11.028 --> 00:02:14.518
I took genetics classes in college,
consulted for

00:02:14.518 --> 00:02:19.319
genetics companies, and
I'm studying it again, here at Stanford.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:21.897
And I want to share with
you what I've learned.

00:02:23.722 --> 00:02:28.398
First and foremost,
this stuff is really complicated.

00:02:28.398 --> 00:02:35.041
A big part of who we are is our DNA and
our environment.

00:02:35.041 --> 00:02:41.140
Our DNA is the biological matter
that carries our genetic code.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:46.132
And our environment is where we grew up,
who we surrounded ourselves with, and

00:02:46.132 --> 00:02:48.379
what events happened in our lives.

00:02:48.379 --> 00:02:52.042
It would be great if we
could hold DNA constant, and

00:02:52.042 --> 00:02:58.197
adjust our environment to see which would
be more powerful, in which circumstance.

00:02:58.197 --> 00:03:00.988
But unfortunately, it's not that simple.

00:03:00.988 --> 00:03:07.098
We can ever truly disentangle our DNA
from our environment because they're

00:03:07.098 --> 00:03:13.029
constantly interacting, and
changing each other, and they change us.

00:03:13.029 --> 00:03:16.475
So it's futile to ask
which is more important,

00:03:16.475 --> 00:03:21.389
because it is only through their
interaction, that they can change

00:03:21.389 --> 00:03:26.404
things like our health, human behavior,
and who we are and become.

00:03:26.404 --> 00:03:30.595
So I want to walk through three really
complex examples to explain how

00:03:30.595 --> 00:03:31.410
this works.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:36.208
And I'm going to break them down
into their most essential parts.

00:03:36.208 --> 00:03:41.052
Let's start with an example
that affects our mental health,

00:03:41.052 --> 00:03:43.950
by looking at a study on depression.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:49.429
Scientists identified a genetic
profile linked to depression.

00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:54.678
For simplicity, let's call it D gene.

00:03:54.678 --> 00:03:59.102
Scientist looked at kids with D gene,
and without D gene,

00:03:59.102 --> 00:04:03.273
to see why some develop depression and
others don't.

00:04:03.273 --> 00:04:08.226
They found that kids with D gene,
who had negative experiences,

00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:12.460
such as the loss of a family member or
a serious illness,

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:17.793
were two times as likely as kids
without D gene to develop depression.

00:04:17.793 --> 00:04:24.372
But they also found that kids with D gene,
who had not had negative experiences,

00:04:24.372 --> 00:04:29.916
were no more likely than kids without
D gene to develop depression.

00:04:29.916 --> 00:04:35.177
The lack of an experiential factor
essentially equalized the chances that

00:04:35.177 --> 00:04:40.279
kids would develop depression,
regardless of whether they had D gene.

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:46.972
And we also know that 50% of people with
depression have no genetic history for it.

00:04:46.972 --> 00:04:51.234
Which means you can have D gene,
and never develop depression, or

00:04:51.234 --> 00:04:55.660
you can live with depression, and
have no genetic connection to it.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.367
So now that we've talked about
an example in human health,

00:04:59.367 --> 00:05:04.189
let's talk about an example in human
behavior, specifically, friendly or

00:05:04.189 --> 00:05:07.540
pro-social behavior versus
aggressive behavior.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:12.037
Scientists thought that they'd
identified a gene linked to aggression.

00:05:12.037 --> 00:05:16.228
Let's call it A gene.

00:05:16.228 --> 00:05:19.894
They looked at people with
A gene to study the prevalence

00:05:19.894 --> 00:05:22.785
of friendly versus aggressive behaviors.

00:05:22.785 --> 00:05:27.827
And first, they looked at people with
A gene, who had had aversive life events,

00:05:27.827 --> 00:05:31.324
such as neglect,
in the first two years of their lives.

00:05:31.324 --> 00:05:35.939
They found a statistically significant
relationship between aversive life

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:38.713
events and exhibiting aggressive behavior.

00:05:38.713 --> 00:05:43.420
But interestingly,
they also found that people with A gene,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.136
who had had positive experiences,

00:05:46.136 --> 00:05:51.675
actually exhibited more friendly
behavior than people without A gene.

00:05:51.675 --> 00:05:55.779
So had scientists really
found the aggression gene, or

00:05:55.779 --> 00:05:58.322
had they found the friendly gene?

00:05:58.322 --> 00:06:02.752
A gene, itself, doesn't determine
whether we'll be friendly or aggressive.

00:06:02.752 --> 00:06:06.694
Our environment plays a huge role as well.

00:06:06.694 --> 00:06:11.569
When it comes to health and
behavior, scientists, presumably,

00:06:11.569 --> 00:06:14.701
are looking to get to
the root of a problem,

00:06:14.701 --> 00:06:18.718
to improve our individual and
societal well-being.

00:06:18.718 --> 00:06:22.263
But I want to switch gears,
and talk about something that

00:06:22.263 --> 00:06:26.873
shouldn't be viewed as a problem, but
is often studied in the same way.

00:06:26.873 --> 00:06:31.626
So now let's get back to
what got this whole genetics

00:06:31.626 --> 00:06:35.199
research party started, sexuality.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:40.103
For decades, scientists have tried to find

00:06:40.103 --> 00:06:44.085
the gay gene without much success.

00:06:44.085 --> 00:06:48.992
They're having trouble testing
their hypothesis because first and

00:06:48.992 --> 00:06:53.909
foremost, multiple genes are involved
in any complex human trait.

00:06:53.909 --> 00:06:56.659
Scientists know that a minimum of 2, but

00:06:56.659 --> 00:07:00.206
up to 16 genes could influence
our eye color alone.

00:07:00.206 --> 00:07:03.767
So how could one determine sexuality?

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:07.085
And even when we know
the genes that are involved,

00:07:07.085 --> 00:07:09.931
our environment plays a huge role as well.

00:07:09.931 --> 00:07:14.912
But it's not just testing the hypothesis
that's a problem, it's defining it,

00:07:14.912 --> 00:07:17.088
that's been challenging as well.

00:07:17.088 --> 00:07:21.097
Because what are scientists
trying to identify

00:07:21.097 --> 00:07:24.410
by finding a gay gene anyway, and why?

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:28.573
Is it who we're capable
of being attracted to,

00:07:28.573 --> 00:07:33.056
who we're capable of loving,
or how we identify?

00:07:33.056 --> 00:07:38.528
When it comes to sexuality,
there are so many variables involved,

00:07:38.528 --> 00:07:44.576
and they're complex, fluid,
non-binary, and socially influenced.

00:07:44.576 --> 00:07:50.516
So this complicates scientists ability
to even define terms like sexuality,

00:07:50.516 --> 00:07:55.653
gender, love, attraction,
what's genetic, and what's not.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:03.175
And yet somehow, when I was 17 years old,

00:08:03.175 --> 00:08:08.201
I somehow expected those scientists and

00:08:08.201 --> 00:08:11.762
their studies to define me.

00:08:11.762 --> 00:08:16.615
So why did I do it,
why did I Google gay gene?

00:08:16.615 --> 00:08:19.233
Why did I think it was so simple?

00:08:19.233 --> 00:08:22.958
Why did I need an explanation for
my feelings?

00:08:22.958 --> 00:08:25.909
Why did I need to know why?

00:08:25.909 --> 00:08:29.339
And why does anyone need to know why?

00:08:29.339 --> 00:08:35.790
I realize that I Googled gay gene to
begin with, because my whole life,

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:41.274
I've gotten question,
after question, after question,

00:08:41.274 --> 00:08:46.219
that reinforced that our
genetics made a real family,

00:08:46.219 --> 00:08:50.414
that only genetic
connection is legitimate,

00:08:50.414 --> 00:08:54.953
that our genetics could
predestine who I become.

00:08:57.165 --> 00:09:01.747
So we shouldn't use words like genetic or
biological or

00:09:01.747 --> 00:09:07.987
natural as synonyms for normal,
because that's how I internalized them,

00:09:07.987 --> 00:09:13.836
and it sent me on a 10 year journey
to study something that I ultimately

00:09:13.836 --> 00:09:20.077
find really fascinating, but that
started as me seeking an explanation for

00:09:20.077 --> 00:09:24.388
my feelings,
as if they were a problem to be solved.

00:09:24.388 --> 00:09:29.025
Now I know that my family,
my sexuality, my identity,

00:09:29.025 --> 00:09:35.057
they're not problems at all, and
they don't require an explanation.

00:09:35.057 --> 00:09:41.245
No one should have to Google
anything to justify their existence,

00:09:41.245 --> 00:09:47.653
because whoever you are, is okay,
whether it's found to be true for

00:09:47.653 --> 00:09:53.527
you genetically from the beginning,
or becomes true for you.

00:09:53.527 --> 00:09:59.411
If I've learned anything from all of this,
it's that our families,

00:09:59.411 --> 00:10:03.300
our identities, our health, our behavior,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:09.106
basically anything about us,
cannot be reduced to genetics alone.

00:10:09.106 --> 00:10:14.691
Because humans are so
much more complicated than that.

00:10:17.045 --> 00:10:22.544
So yes,
genetic discoveries have tremendous

00:10:22.544 --> 00:10:27.056
potential to benefit humankind, but

00:10:27.056 --> 00:10:31.859
genetics does not define our humanity.

00:10:31.859 --> 00:10:39.138
Not my mom's, not my brother's,
not mine, and not yours.

00:10:39.138 --> 00:10:41.101
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:10:41.101 --> 00:10:46.001
[MUSIC]

